`tiktoken` is imported unconditionally; if it’s not installed in the environment where the packaged plugin is used, running this script will crash immediately. If this is intended to be an optional developer tool, import `tiktoken` lazily (inside `count_tokens`) and/or catch ImportError with a clear message about how to install the dependency.
```suggestion

def count_tokens(text: str, model: str = "gpt-4") -> int:
    """Count tokens using OpenAI tokenizer."""
    try:
        import tiktoken
    except ImportError as exc:
        raise RuntimeError(
            "tiktoken is required to run this benchmark. "
            "Install it with: pip install tiktoken"
        ) from exc

    try:
```

I ran this against `de876ff` in a clean `golang:1.26` container.

`proxy/providers/adapter.go:434` switches header forwarding from an allowlist (`copyIfPresent`, 9 named headers) to `req.Header.Clone()` plus a denylist of hop-by-hop and `x-cave-*` names. `Cookie`, `Referer`, and `X-Forwarded-For` match none of the denylist entries, so they now forward to the upstream LLM provider unchanged. On `main` (`15581d1`) they were stripped, since the old allowlist never copied them.

```go
b := Base{Provider: "openai"}
req.Header.Set("cookie", "session=super-secret-proxy-session")
out, _ := b.SanitizeAndMapHeaders(req.Context(), req, Credential{Key: "UPSTREAM-KEY"}, nil)
// de876ff:  out.Get("cookie") == "session=super-secret-proxy-session"
// 15581d1:  out.Get("cookie") == ""
```

The PR's own new test only checks `x-cave-*`, `connection`, and two named hop-by-hop headers; it doesn't cover `Cookie` or other client-context headers the old allowlist implicitly dropped. I haven't checked whether any real caller of this proxy actually sends a `Cookie` header, only that the sanitizer no longer strips one if present. Adding those names to the denylist would close this without losing the provider-header fix.

I checked this against the published package and the central claim doesn't hold — `session.idle` **is** a V2 event. Not changing the code.

`@opencode-ai/plugin` has no 2.x line; the V2 package is `@opencode/plugin`, whose `latest` is `2.0.7` (matching the 2.0.5/2.0.7 in #1083). Installing it and following the types:

```
@opencode/plugin/dist/promise/event.d.ts
  EventDomain extends Pick<EventApi, "subscribe">
@opencode/client/dist/promise/generated/client.d.ts:182
  event: { subscribe: (requestOptions?) => AsyncIterable<EventSubscribeOutput> }
@opencode/client/dist/promise/generated/types.d.ts:7785
  export type EventSubscribeOutput = V2Event;
```

and `V2Event` (line 3279) includes `SessionIdle` alongside `SessionExecutionStarted | SessionExecutionSucceeded | SessionExecutionFailed | SessionExecutionInterrupted`. `SessionIdle` is declared at line 1733 as:

```ts
export type SessionIdle = {
    id: string; created: number; metadata?: {...};
    type: "session.idle";
    location?: LocationRef;
    data: { sessionID: string };
};
```

That is exactly the shape `handleEvent` reads (`event.type`, `event.data.sessionID`), so `session.idle` reaches the subscription and Stop is recorded. The fixture isn't inventing an event name.

You're right that `session.execution.*` exists and is new in V2, but those are per-execution boundaries, not the session-idle boundary the V1 integration recorded Stop at. Mapping Stop onto them would change semantics — it'd fire once per execution rather than when the session goes quiet. Keeping the idle boundary preserves V1 parity, which is what this PR is for.

If you have a source showing `session.idle` is emitted by the V1 server but not the V2 one despite being in the 2.0.7 event union, I'll happily reopen this — the type union is the best primary source I can reach offline.

